## Proof

Let $X = \mathbb{R}^{\mathbb{R}}$ with the product topology. A basic open set in the product topology has the form
$$U = \{f \in X : f(x_i) \in U_i \text{ for } i = 1, \dots, n\},$$
where $x_1, \dots, x_n \in \mathbb{R}$ are finitely many points and each $U_i \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ is a nonempty open set. (Equivalently, $U = \prod_{x \in \mathbb{R}} V_x$ where $V_x = U_i$ if $x = x_i$ for some $i$, and $V_x = \mathbb{R}$ otherwise.)

### $A$ is dense in $X$

Let $U = \{f : f(x_i) \in U_i,\ i=1,\dots,n\}$ be any nonempty basic open set. Each $U_i$ is a nonempty open subset of $\mathbb{R}$, so each contains a rational number $q_i \in \mathbb{Q} \cap U_i$. Define
$$f(x) = \begin{cases} q_i & \text{if } x = x_i \text{ for some } i \in \{1,\dots,n\},\\ 0 & \text{otherwise.} \end{cases}$$
Then $f(x) \in \mathbb{Q}$ for every $x \in \mathbb{R}$, and $f(x) = 0$ for all but finitely many $x$ (namely, at most the $n$ points $x_1,\dots,x_n$). Hence $f \in A$. Moreover $f(x_i) = q_i \in U_i$ for each $i$, so $f \in U$. Thus $A \cap U \neq \emptyset$ for every basic open set $U$, which means $A$ is dense in $X$.

### $B$ is not dense in $X$

Consider the basic open set
$$U = \{f \in X : f(0) \in (1, 2)\}.$$
This is a nonempty open set (e.g., the constant function $f \equiv 1.5$ belongs to it). However, every $f \in B$ satisfies $f(0) = 0$ because $0 \in \mathbb{Q}$. Since $0 \notin (1,2)$, no element of $B$ lies in $U$. Therefore $B \cap U = \emptyset$, and $B$ is not dense in $X$.

### Conclusion

$A$ is dense in $X$, while $B$ is not dense in $X$.

